/query?manifest=7606ede1164df321a11fa37e03c1309bd8ec65a37d50c5dd220f3a65f910ab03&node=support.endpoint_chord_area&relation=direct","prerequisite_uses":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=7606ede1164df321a11fa37e03c1309bd8ec65a37d50c5dd220f3a65f910ab03&node=support.endpoint_chord_area&relation=prerequisites","prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=7606ede1164df321a11fa37e03c1309bd8ec65a37d50c5dd220f3a65f910ab03&view=arguments&node=support.endpoint_chord_area&assumptions=true"},"neighborhood_href":"/api/v1/neighborhood?manifest=7606ede1164df321a11fa37e03c1309bd8ec65a37d50c5dd220f3a65f910ab03&ref=sha256%3A3592e6a1f4e5ac9cf05e86d1dac98fabd5ac5f25ba57c4ce6e79c2b2f110d0db%2Fnodes%2Fsupport.endpoint_chord_area","record":{"assumption_ids":[],"boundary":"An ordinary one-dimensional calculus lemma with its own quantified hypotheses. It applies to toy curves as well as diffusion moments and carries no minimizer, PDE or stochastic premise. Strict convexity gives q′(t) below its endpoint chord for r<t<s; the chord is at most one, and continuity makes the integrated deficit strictly positive. The source uses this step inside a conditional full-support argument. This node does not assert a newly extracted Lean declaration or independently accepted formal check.","citation_ids":["support_closure_source"],"evidence_ids":["endpoint_chord_area_warrant_20261024"],"id":"support.endpoint_chord_area","kind":"claim","label":"The integrated endpoint-chord inequality","locator":"variation.tex:223–234; calculus step in the bounded-gap proof","source_id":"support_tex_variation","statement":"Let r<s and q be continuously differentiable on [r,s]. If q′ is strictly convex on [r,s] and q′(r),q′(s)<=1, then q(s)-q(r)<s-r.","status":"unverified"},"ref":"sha256:3592e6a1f4e5ac9cf05e86d1dac98fabd5ac5f25ba57c4ce6e79c2b2f110d0db/nodes/support.endpoint_chord_area","relations":[{"derived":false,"family":"arguments","from":"sha256:3592e6a1f4e5ac9cf05e86d1dac98fabd5ac5f25ba57c4ce6e79c2b2f110d0db/nodes/support.no_